window.docContents[2].resolve({"/Hoare/#Hoare-Logic--Hoare___-Hoare-Logic___-Part-I--Proof-Rules--Sequencing-___-Assignment":{"contents":"Here's an example of a program involving both sequencing and\nassignment.  Note the use of hoare_seq in conjunction with\nhoare_consequence_pre and apply's metavariables.\n\ntheorem hoare_asgn_example3 (a : Aexp) (n : Nat) :\n    {{a = n}}\n      X := ~a;\n      skip;\n    {{X = n}} := by\n  apply hoare_seq\n  · -- right part of seq\n    apply hoare_skip\n  · -- left part of seq\n    apply hoare_consequence_pre\n    · apply hoare_asgn\n    · assertion_auto\n\n\n\n\nInformally, a nice way of displaying a proof using the sequencing\nrule is as a \"decorated program\" where the intermediate assertion\nQ is written between c1 and c2:\n\n         {{ a = n }}\nX := a\n         {{ X = n }};    <--- decoration for Q\nskip\n         {{ X = n }}\n\n\nWe'll come back to the idea of decorated programs in much more\ndetail in the next chapter.\n\n\n\n","context":"Hoare Logic\u0009Hoare: Hoare Logic, Part I\u0009Proof Rules","header":"4.4.7. Sequencing + Assignment","id":"/Hoare/#Hoare-Logic--Hoare___-Hoare-Logic___-Part-I--Proof-Rules--Sequencing-___-Assignment"}});